1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Steady-state performance has dominated our
recent episodes: GC, JIT warmup,
safepoints, heap shape.

2
00:00:08,119 --> 00:00:20,600
Then you deploy to a scale-to-zero
environment, or you restart pods on every
deploy, or you run short-lived CLI tools
that should feel instant. Suddenly the
question is not p99 after warmup.

3
00:00:20,719 --> 00:00:26,399
The question is how long from process
start until the app can do useful work.
Startup is a feature.

4
00:00:26,519 --> 00:00:37,519
Teams that only optimize the warmed steady
state discover that truth the first time
readiness probes fail in a rolling deploy.
Look at this bash for a moment. That
command looks simple.

5
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:46,799
Behind it, the JVM loads classes, verifies
bytecode, initializes classes, runs static
initializers, and begins with a cold JIT.

6
00:00:46,920 --> 00:01:00,480
Your framework likely scans the classpath,
wires beans, opens connection pools, and
warms caches you did not ask to warm at
boot. Classloading, verification, and
initialization dominate many cold starts.

7
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:11,120
A fat classpath makes that worse — every
jar is more work before business logic
matters. Ignoring classpath bloat while
"optimizing startup" is rearranging
furniture during a flood.

8
00:01:11,239 --> 00:01:15,119
Heavy work in static initializers is a
classic self-inflicted wound.

9
00:01:15,239 --> 00:01:27,519
A class loads because something touched
it, its static initializer hits a database
or builds a giant cache, and first-request
latency includes an accidental boot
ritual. Lazy initialization helps —
carefully.

10
00:01:27,639 --> 00:01:41,639
Lazy means "not at class init time," not
"no concurrency story." Initialize on
first use with clear thread safety, or on
a deliberate lifecycle hook you can see in
metrics and logs. Distinguish three clocks
teams mix up.

11
00:01:41,759 --> 00:01:49,719
Wall time to process start is not wall
time to readiness, which is not wall time
to stable p99 after JIT warmup.

12
00:01:49,839 --> 00:01:55,599
A service can pass a shallow readiness
probe while still loading classes lazily
on the first real traffic wave.

13
00:01:55,719 --> 00:02:05,519
Another can be "slow to start" because
static initializers do work that should be
a deliberate warm step with metrics.
Measure each clock separately or you
optimize the wrong one.

14
00:02:05,639 --> 00:02:16,959
The platform offers acceleration once you
understand the cost drivers. CDS and
AppCDS archive class metadata so
subsequent runs spend less time loading
and linking common classes.

15
00:02:17,079 --> 00:02:27,919
Ahead-of-time compilation and Graal native
image trade some dynamic JIT flexibility
and reflection freedom for a native binary
with faster startup and a different peak-
performance profile.

16
00:02:28,039 --> 00:02:38,120
CRaC — Coordinated Restore at Checkpoint —
aims at snapshotted restarts: warm a
process, checkpoint, restore later into a
ready state. Each technique has a fit.
None is free.

17
00:02:38,239 --> 00:02:49,799
Native image constraints surprise teams
that relied on runtime reflection
everywhere. Checkpoint restore needs
discipline about what state is safe to
freeze. Classpath bloat has a human story.

18
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:56,479
Someone adds a starter for one annotation
processor, transitive jars multiply, boot
scanning grows.

19
00:02:56,599 --> 00:03:04,479
Native image later looks attractive
because startup hurt — but the cheaper
first move may be deleting unused
dependencies and delaying initialization.

20
00:03:04,599 --> 00:03:09,840
AOT and CRaC are powerful; they are not
substitutes for a lean boot path.

21
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:22,240
For serverless-style or scale-to-zero
Java, startup becomes part of product
latency — then CDS, native image, or
checkpoint/restore move from interesting
to necessary.

22
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:33,840
For a long-lived monolith that restarts
monthly, spend energy on steady-state SLOs
first. Fit the tool to the deployment
model, the same way we fitted collectors
to SLOs.

23
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:46,000
Ways to improve startup, said as an
engineer: reduce classpath and
initialization work first, use CDS or
AppCDS where it pays, lean on lazy and
deliberate lifecycle init,

24
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:51,400
and consider native image or
checkpoint/restore when the deployment
model demands fast bring-up.

25
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:59,680
Doing heavy work in static initializers
and shipping enormous classpaths are the
mistakes that make exotic tools look
necessary before you have earned them.

26
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:11,680
We closed the JVM's life cycle from cold
start through steady-state pauses. The
remaining gap is telling that arc as one
coherent interview story — from load to
diagnosis — without buzzword bingo.
